    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_6192619b89db11f34864bad13a167a58d9827aebfb5d279aa38bd1da8a8a7dda",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Rankin-Selberg unfolding first converts the constant Fourier term of a rapidly decaying automorphic function into a Mellin transform whose completed-Eisenstein singularities yield zeta-zero-indexed horocycle errors; Zagier subtraction then preserves this mechanism for moderate power-log cusp behavior, and the non-tachyonic string partition function supplies exactly such a moderate-growth automorphic input. Under the proper-time/cutoff dictionary, its level-matched constant term becomes a smoothed graded state count, so the same zero-indexed powers constrain the cutoff dependence of the spectrum and relate its leading term to the modular vacuum energy.",
