    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_cc59fc52b62b19834f4e8326414e2f44ac53cf8d537b30dce4f950b7f3b426d3",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The general zero-indexed expansion does not guarantee visible oscillations for every automorphic function: the discriminant example has a normalized horocycle error whose observed frequencies approximately recover the first zeta-zero ordinates under the RH form, whereas the moderate-growth theta example has a slower deterministic square-root correction and an explicit Rankin-Selberg residue calculation making every zero coefficient vanish. Together the examples show that the chosen automorphic observable can transmit, mask, or annihilate spectral information.",
