    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_f353d366cc72d4d104eac8244bd2cd59a21b4c79f3c35d6b6dea403d2a9c7599",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The cutoff-weighted graded state sum is analytically powerful in two directions: it constrains asymptotic boson-fermion imbalance and inverts to a finite-state approximation of the modular vacuum energy. Yet string-state Hagedorn growth makes that representation computationally ill-conditioned: reaching a given cutoff requires levels of order the fourth power of the cutoff and cancellation between exponentially large bosonic and fermionic contributions. The rational Scherk-Schwarz model exposes the needed alternating graded coefficients algebraically, illustrating how a compact asymptotic relation can coexist with difficult finite reconstruction.",
